Description: Optional read-only debugfs dump of EC RAM 0x00-0xff (ecram)
 TESTKIT-DESIGN section 12 item 9 (operator decision 2026-10-10): EC RAM
 snapshots for TESTSPEC C1/T6 needed mode A, i.e. module unloaded and the
 userspace research tool ec_probe doing raw port I/O on 0x62/0x66 (ioperm,
 outside the driver lock, checklist C2). This adds the dump to the driver,
 through the driver's own locked EC read path, so no userspace port I/O is
 needed while the driver is loaded.
 .
 Gate: unchanged from 0003. The file <debugfs>/minisforum_n5_it5571/ecram
 (mode 0400, seq_file, read only, not seekable, no write/ioctl/mmap) is
 created next to dcr only with debug_regs=1 (0444, default N) and only on
 a matched board profile whose DMI sys_vendor is "Micro Computer (HK) Tech
 Limited"; force=1 or a vendor mismatch ignore debug_regs=1 with a
 warning, as for dcr. Both files are created or none (a failed create
 removes the directory). debug_regs=N: no debugfs entry, no EC RAM dump.
 .
 Access: each of the 256 bytes is one call of n5_ec_read_locked() (ACPI EC
 read command 0x80 + address on 0x62/0x66, the transaction used for the
 four temperatures since upstream) under data->lock, with the 0005 wait
 bound (100 ms per status wait, resync after a timeout) and the 0005
 per-access budget (N5_ACCESS_BUDGET_MS). The lock is dropped between
 bytes, so a dump never holds it longer than one temperature read (at most
 3 waits = 300 ms plus one resync sleep <= 20 ms); the C9 bound of 0005 is
 not extended. The lock is taken with mutex_lock_killable().
 .
 Dump budget N5_ECRAM_BUDGET_MS = 2000 ms from the start of the dump. A
 byte is started only while at least N5_ECRAM_BYTE_US = 3 x 100 ms + 2 x
 10 ms = 320 ms of it are left (checked under the lock), so the budget
 never cuts a transaction after its command byte: no resync flag, no extra
 cost for the next temperature read, no "EC timeout" line without an EC
 fault. Otherwise the dump ends before any port access with -ETIMEDOUT and
 the ratelimited warning "ecram dump aborted before EC RAM 0xNN: less
 than 320000 us of the 2000 ms dump budget left"; it contains neither "EC
 timeout" nor "timeout" (whether TESTSPEC K1 counts it is the operator's
 decision). A status wait that runs out inside a transaction is a real EC
 timeout and logged by 0005 as before. Wall time of one dump <= 2 s plus
 one foreign lock hold (<= 1 s + 20 ms) plus scheduling delay. The values
 are bounds, not measurements: EC response times are not measured; the
 duration of a dump on the device is to be measured before adoption.
 Address range and length are compile-time constants; there is no EC RAM
 write (command 0x81 is not used anywhere), no offset or length input
 (G3).
 .
 Error semantics, all or nothing: the first failed byte ends the dump and
 read() returns its error, nothing is printed: -ETIMEDOUT (status wait
 inside a transaction, or dump budget left < 320 ms before a byte),
 -ENODEV (dump running when unbind/unload starts: stopping, see Teardown;
 or a read after .shutdown: dead), -EINTR (fatal signal, also while
 waiting for the lock). Unlike ec_probe, which prints "??" and goes on, a
 byte is never reported as missing: continuing against an EC that does
 not answer would cost up to 300 ms per byte and repeat the timeout
 warning 256 times, and a dump with holes is not comparable byte by byte.
 Errors from the debugfs core, not from this driver: a file descriptor
 opened before unbind and read after it: -EIO (debugfs_file_get() on the
 removed dentry); open() after removal: -ENOENT. While any process holds
 ecram (or dcr) open, the module cannot be unloaded: the debugfs open
 takes a module reference (fops .owner = THIS_MODULE), modprobe -r fails
 with "Module is in use"; this is not a driver error. Unbind does not
 take a module reference and proceeds.
 .
 Not seekable: own .open with nonseekable_open() + single_open(). With
 DEFINE_SHOW_ATTRIBUTE, lseek() or a pread() at an offset other than the
 current position made seq_file run show() again, i.e. a whole new dump
 with new values that could fail half-way through the file. Now lseek()
 and pread() return -ESPIPE; show() runs once per open (on the first
 read), sequential partial reads are served from its buffer.
 .
 Output: 16 rows "%02x: %*ph" ("00: 00 00 00 0e ..."), lower case, no
 trailing blank, 16 x 52 = 832 bytes. tools/f8a extract_rows (2 address
 digits, case insensitive) parses the rows, but load_ecram() only reads
 them between the markers "=== BEGIN EC_RAM n ===" / "=== END EC_RAM n
 ===" (f8a-snapshot.sh); a tool that reads ecram must write that wrapper.
 The bytes are read over about the dump's duration with hwmon accesses in
 between; it is not one instant (neither is mode A).
 .
 Teardown: order unchanged from 0003/0004 (debugfs devres action
 registered after the hwmon device, runs first; then hwmon; then the
 restore action sets data->dead; then the port regions are released).
 New: the debugfs action now gets data instead of the dentry and sets
 data->stopping (WRITE_ONCE) before debugfs_remove_recursive(); a running
 dump checks it under the lock before every byte and ends with -ENODEV.
 debugfs_remove_recursive() therefore waits for at most the byte in
 progress (<= 300 ms + 20 ms resync) plus the foreign lock holds queued
 ahead of the reader (each <= 1 s + 20 ms: hwmon access budget + resync;
 one in the normal case), about 1.34 s plus scheduling delay, instead of
 up to a whole dump (2 s + one foreign hold + 20 ms, about 3.3 s). The
 restore budget (<= 4 s, only for touched channels) follows as before.
 Without stopping, -ENODEV from dead was practically unreachable on
 unbind/unload, because debugfs is removed before dead is set; it remains
 the error after .shutdown.
 .
 Fairness: N parallel ecram readers (root only) queue for the lock between
 their bytes; hwmon accesses then wait for up to N byte holds (N x 320 ms)
 instead of one.
 .
 MODULE_VERSION set to 0.2.1+sr2~rc1 for the measurement build, so that
 modinfo tells it from the sr1 build; the srcversion is recorded in the
 measurement protocol in addition (the draft may change before adoption).
 On adoption this hunk moves to 0001 (see patches-draft/README.md).
 Header change notice date updated (GPL-2.0 2(a)).
 .
 Checklist: G3 (fixed range, read only, not seekable), G5 (parameter off
 by default, 0400, vendor-gated), G6 (warnings ratelimited), B6 unchanged
 (no Super-I/O access added), C1/C2 (whole transaction per byte under
 data->lock), C3 n/a (no cache), C4/C5/C7/C8 (removal order, stopping,
 -ENODEV/-EIO/-ENOENT), C9 (lock hold per byte, dump budget, teardown
 wait), E1 unchanged (no write path; restore order untouched), H1 (same
 read sequence as the ec_probe dump in mode A, now repeatable while the
 driver is loaded; covered by M-E6), H5 (MODULE_VERSION).
 .
 Tested: applies with --fuzz=0 on 0001-0007; W=1, sparse, smatch, checkpatch
 --no-tree -f against 7.0.14-20-pve and 7.0.12-1-pve. Not tested on
 hardware: ecram vs. f8a-snapshot mode A dump, duration of one dump,
 /proc/ioports and kernel log during a read, read during unbind, unload
 with an open reader, lseek/pread -ESPIPE.
